"{\n  \"entities\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Hoaxes\",\n      \"type\": \"Concept\",\n      \"description\": \"Hoaxes are deliberate deceptions or fabrications designed to mislead or trick people, often for amusement, profit, or to challenge established beliefs. They have been a part of human history for centuries and can occur in various fields such as art, science, and media.\",\n      \"attributes\": {\n        \"Purpose\": \"To mislead, trick, or challenge established beliefs\",\n        \"Duration\": \"Centuries\",\n        \"Impact\": \"Shaping historical narratives, testing the boundaries of truth and fiction, and highlighting the need for critical thinking\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Piltdown Man\",\n      \"type\": \"Hoax\",\n      \"description\": \"The Piltdown Man was a notorious hoax involving a supposed missing link between humans and apes, discovered in England in 1912. It misled the scientific community for decades and fueled debate and skepticism about the validity of evolutionary theory.\",\n      \"attributes\": {\n        \"Discovery Date\": \"1912\",\n        \"Location\": \"England\",\n        \"Nature\": \"Supposed missing link between humans and apes\",\n        \"Duration of Deception\": \"Decades\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"1869\",\n      \"type\": \"Year\",\n      \"description\": \"1869 is the year in which the Cardiff Giant hoax was staged, marking a significant event in the history of American hoaxes and public deception.\",\n      \"attributes\": {\n        \"Century\": \"19th Century\",\n        \"Decade\": \"1860s\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Van Meegeren\\\"s Forgeries\",\n      \"type\": \"Art Hoax\",\n      \"description\": \"Han van Meegeren was a Dutch painter and forger who gained notoriety for creating and selling fake Vermeer paintings during the 1930s and 1940s. His forgeries were so convincing that they fooled art experts and collectors, including Nazi leader Hermann Göring.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"War of the Worlds Broadcast\",\n      \"type\": \"Media Hoax\",\n      \"description\": \"The War of the Worlds radio broadcast by Orson Welles in 1938 was a dramatization of H.G. Wells\\\" novel about a Martian invasion, presented as a series of news bulletins. It led to widespread panic and confusion as many listeners believed it to be real.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Cardiff Giant\",\n      \"type\": \"Archaeological Hoax\",\n      \"description\": \"The Cardiff Giant was a 10-foot-tall petrified man \\\"discovered\\\" in Cardiff, New York, in 1869. It was later revealed to be a sculpture created by George Hull, who sought to profit from the public\\\"s fascination with archaeology and giant mythologies.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Sokal Affair\",\n      \"type\": \"Academic Hoax\",\n      \"description\": \"The Sokal Affair of 1996 involved physicist Alan Sokal submitting a fake paper titled \\\"Transgressing the Boundaries: Towards a Transformative Hermeneutics of Quantum Gravity\\\" to the journal Social Text. The journal published the nonsensical article without realizing its lack of scientific rigor.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Scientific Community\",\n      \"type\": \"Community\",\n      \"description\": \"The scientific community is a group of individuals who engage in scientific research and share their findings.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Evolutionary Theory\",\n      \"type\": \"Scientific Theory\",\n      \"description\": \"Evolutionary theory is a scientific explanation for the diversity of life and the changes in species over time.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Hoax\",\n      \"type\": \"Event\",\n      \"description\": \"The exposure of a hoax is an event where fraudulent information or objects are revealed to be false.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Historical Hoaxes\",\n      \"type\": \"Category\",\n      \"description\": \"Historical hoaxes are significant deceptions that have occurred throughout history, often with lasting impacts on society and culture.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Theories of Human Evolution\",\n      \"type\": \"Scientific Theory\",\n      \"description\": \"Theories of human evolution are scientific explanations for the development and diversification of the human species over time.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Debate and Skepticism\",\n      \"type\": \"Social Phenomenon\",\n      \"description\": \"Debate and skepticism are critical components of scientific and public discourse, often driving further investigation and clarification.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    }\n  ],\n  \"relations\": [\n    [\n      \"Hoaxes\",\n      \"Includes\",\n      \"Van Meegeren\\\"s Forgeries\",\n      \"Van Meegeren\\\"s forgeries are a part of the broader category of hoaxes, specifically in the art world.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Hoaxes\",\n      \"Includes\",\n      \"War of the Worlds Broadcast\",\n      \"The War of the Worlds broadcast is a significant example of a media hoax that incited mass hysteria and highlighted the power of mass media to manipulate public perception.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Hoaxes\",\n      \"Includes\",\n      \"Piltdown Man\",\n      \"The Piltdown Man hoax is a notable scientific deception that challenged existing theories of human evolution and fueled debate and skepticism about the validity of evolutionary theory.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Hoaxes\",\n      \"Includes\",\n      \"Cardiff Giant\",\n      \"The Cardiff Giant is an example of an archaeological hoax that attracted massive crowds and generated significant revenue before being exposed.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Hoaxes\",\n      \"Includes\",\n      \"Sokal Affair\",\n      \"The Sokal Affair is a famous academic hoax that sparked a debate about the credibility of postmodernist scholarship and the importance of intellectual rigor in academic research.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Piltdown Man\",\n      \"Misled\",\n      \"Scientific Community\",\n      \"The Piltdown Man hoax misled the scientific community for decades, causing them to accept a false evolutionary link.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Piltdown Man\",\n      \"Fueled Debate About\",\n      \"Evolutionary Theory\",\n      \"The Piltdown Man hoax fueled debate and skepticism about the validity of evolutionary theory, challenging established scientific beliefs.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Piltdown Man\",\n      \"Exposed As\",\n      \"Hoax\",\n      \"The Piltdown Man was eventually exposed as a hoax, revealing the fraudulent nature of the supposed fossil.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Piltdown Man\",\n      \"Part Of\",\n      \"Historical Hoaxes\",\n      \"The Piltdown Man is one of the most infamous hoaxes in history, contributing to the broader category of historical hoaxes.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Piltdown Man\",\n      \"Misled\",\n      \"Scientific Community\",\n      \"The Piltdown Man hoax misled the scientific community for decades, causing significant confusion and misdirection in the field of human evolution.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Piltdown Man\",\n      \"Challenged\",\n      \"Theories of Human Evolution\",\n      \"The Piltdown Man was claimed to challenge existing theories of human evolution, leading to widespread debate and scrutiny.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Piltdown Man\",\n      \"Fuelled\",\n      \"Debate and Skepticism\",\n      \"The Piltdown Man hoax fueled debate and skepticism about the validity of evolutionary theory, influencing public and scientific discussions.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"1869\",\n      \"Year of Discovery\",\n      \"Cardiff Giant\",\n      \"The Cardiff Giant hoax was staged and \\\"discovered\\\" in 1869.\"\n    ]\n  ]\n}"